today you were going to be learning  about dynamic equilibrium this is quite  a strange feature so it needs some  explaining if we break it down into two  words we have equilibrium which would  mean everything's equal and yet dynamic  would suggest movement so it would  suggest that although everything's equal  things are moving and the best way to  consider that is to imagine running on a  treadmill so you've seen people at gyms  running along for miles and miles and  miles and yet they never appear to move  anywhere because on their machine as  they're running as they're running along  the treadmill is moving in one direction  as they're running in the other so the  equal part is their position because  their position stays the same and yet  they have because of the treadmill moved  and that's a great example of dynamic  equilibrium things appear to be equal  and yet there's lots of movement  occurring now in science this most often  occurs when we see something called a  reversible reaction the most common one  of which is called the harbor process  and it is the method of production of  ammonia but in an example of a  reversible reaction we have our  reactants perhaps a plus B in a reaction  followed by this symbol and then this  might join together to make the compound  a B the symbol here is suggesting that  there is movement in one direction for  words but there is also movement in the  opposite direction backwards i reverse  and that's what makes this a reversible  reaction now so explain further  this means that  a and B are reacting and they're  reacting to form a B but as they react  to form a B a B is just chemically  breaking down to form a plus B again so  in other words we have a plus B making a  B but at the same time a B is reacting  to make a plus B this means that we have  a reversible reaction it's going round  in a cycle so that gives us reversible  that's fine but that's our dynamism we  have things constantly breaking down and  being remade so what makes a dynamic  equilibrium well a fact is that as this  reaction is happening at some point  you're going to end up with a relatively  constant amount of a B because it's  always being made and a constant amount  of a plus B because it's always breaking  down and at that point it may be  something like if I put this down here a  plus B and a B that in a mixture there  might be 50 percent of the chemical is  in the form of a B and 50 percent is in  the form of a B together so that seems  fine we've got this mixture how does  that still make it dynamic though  because we've got 50 percent here and 50  percent here well the fact is although  it looks like this value is constant the  actual individual molecules and atoms  that are making up this a B and this a  plus B are actually changing all the  time  so some of this in any one instance I  will see maybe atom a and atom B as  being separate and then we've got  separate atoms here that are joined up  as a B whereas if I come back in ten  minutes time it might be the atom and  atom B have now combined and over this  side of the equation whereas the a and B  that we had here have now broken down  and gone over to this side I sometimes  view this as being like students with  girlfriends and boyfriends in the year  11 so in my class I'm  may have maybe two or three students  that have boyfriends or girlfriends at  the start of the year and at the end of  the year I might still have two or three  students that are in relationships  however it's not the same students  so although the proportion of students  in a relationship has stayed the same  the actual students that are in that  relationship has changed so we have the  constant proportions which makes it the  equilibrium but it is changing the  individuals which makes it dynamic that  is the dynamic equilibrium  now our really common example that we  see which is the harbor process is the  process by which we get ammonia from air  to make ammonia for fertilizers and in  that case we have nitrogen as a gas plus  hydrogen as a gas and that makes ammonia  as a molecule our compound in the  molecule now the thing with this is that  as you're making the ammonia the ammonia  is also constantly breaking down now in  this case the dynamic equilibrium means  that maybe something like 95% of the  reactants are separate as the ammonia  the nation' and the hydrogen and only 5%  is present as the ammonia and what we do  is we collect that ammonia and we remove  it the proportions here though can be  changed we can change the conditions in  the reaction to try and courage the  forward reaction to be greater than the  reverse reaction and if we can do that  we can encourage this percentage to  increase to maybe 15% and this to  decrease to maybe 85% and therefore we  make this reaction much more profitable  for us as chemists so the proportions  here depend on the conditions that we  apply to the reaction so that is  reversible reactions in summary as our  dynamic equilibrium  in summary dynamic equilibrium is where  we appear to have equal proportions in  reversible reactions and yet the actual  reactants and products are constantly  changing and the most common example of  that we find in the harbour process  where we have nitrogen and hydrogen  being used up to make ammonia but that  ammonia is constantly breaking down to  form nation' and hydrogen again
 

